; DOMAIN: mathematics/logic/model-theory [CLOSED, derived 2026-08-01]; Claim: Model theory studies the relationship between formal languages (signatures, formulas) and their mathematical realizations (structures, models). A structure M for a language L interprets symbols as functions, relations, and constants. The satisfaction relation M |= phi means M satisfies formula phi. Key results include the Compactness Theorem (a theory has a model iff every finite subset does), the Lowenheim-Skolem theorems (downward: elementary submodels of size <= |M|; upward: models of any cardinality >= |L|), the Tarski-Vaught test for elementary substructures, and Morley's categoricity theorem (countable first-order theory categorical in one uncountable cardinal iff categorical in all uncountable cardinals). Stability theory classifies theories by the number of types over sets. Bridges to proof_theory (Godel completeness), category_theory (ultraproducts, elementary classes), and algebraic_geometry (model-theoretic algebraic geometry, Zariski geometries).;; Satisfaction relation: M satisfies formula phi means M interprets symbols and phi holds in M
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Structure $M) (--> Language $L) (--> Interprets $M $L) (--> Formula $phi $L) (--> HoldsIn $M $phi))) (--> Satisfies $M $phi)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Compactness theorem: a theory has a model iff every finite subset has a model
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Theory $T) (--> ForAll $S (FiniteSubset $S $T) (HasModel $S)))) (--> HasModel $T)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Lowenheim-Skolem theorem: if a theory has an infinite model, it has models of every infinite cardinality >= |L|
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Theory $T) (--> Language $L) (--> HasModel $T) (--> InfiniteModel $M $T) (--> Cardinality $k) (--> GreaterEqual $k (Cardinality $L)))) (--> HasModelOfCardinality $T $k)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Tarski-Vaught test: M is an elementary substructure of N iff for every formula phi(x,y) and a in M, if N |= phi(a,b) then b in M
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Structure $M) (--> Structure $N) (--> Substructure $M $N) (--> ForAll $phi (Formula $phi) (ForAll $a (In $a $M) (Exists $b (In $b $N) (Implies (Satisfies $N ($phi $a $b)) (In $b $M))))))) (--> ElementarySubstructure $M $N)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Morley categoricity theorem: countable first-order theory categorical in one uncountable cardinal iff categorical in all uncountable cardinals
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Theory $T) (--> Countable $T) (--> FirstOrder $T) (--> CategoricalIn $T $k1) (--> Uncountable $k1))) (--> CategoricalInAllUncountable $T)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Stability: a theory is stable if the number of types over any set A is bounded by |A|^kappa for some fixed kappa
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Theory $T) (--> ForAll $A (Set $A) (Leq (NumberOfTypes $T $A) (Exponentiation (Cardinality $A) $kappa))))) (--> Stable $T $kappa)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Ultraproduct: product of structures modulo a nonprincipal ultrafilter yields a new structure satisfying Los's theorem
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Structure $M_i) (--> Ultrafilter $U) (--> Nonprincipal $U) (--> LosTheorem $prod))) (--> Ultraproduct $prod (ProductModulo $M_i $U))) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Elementary equivalence: two structures satisfy the same first-order sentences
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Structure $M) (--> Structure $N) (--> ForAll $phi (FirstOrderSentence $phi) (Iff (Satisfies $M $phi) (Satisfies $N $phi))))) (--> ElementaryEquivalent $M $N)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
